film criticism is definitely worthwhile  but let's be honest here  sometimes critics get so caught up in  whether or not a movie is good that they  stop considering whether it's fun to  watch and yes there is a difference from  swashbuckling adventure to action-packed  sci-fi here are some movies that the  critics despised that are actually  pretty awesome if you haven't seen them  yet beware of spoilers ahead hook it  seems like everybody hates hook film  critics have savaged this 1991 Peter Pan  story as embarrassingly excessive  bloated and a huge disappointment and  it's widely considered to be Steven  Spielberg's worst film but maybe that's  because all those critics have grown up  and forgotten what it's like to have an  imagination granted hook is nowhere  close to being one of Spielberg's best  but for a film about childhood it deals  with some surprisingly mature themes  like unrequited love and the  inevitability of death the story stars  of Robin Williams playing Peter Pan as  an adult who changed his name started a  family became a lawyer and totally  forgot about his adventures in Neverland  until his children were kidnapped by  Captain Hook it's a film about  dysfunctional families and abandoned  kids and according to Evans safe Hawk of  birth movies death hook was a very  personal movie for Spielberg a reaction  to his fear is that his heavy workload  was robbing him of time with his  children in addition to the deep stuff  that's going on behind the surface Hook  is pure Spielberg II and entertainment  the sets which are quite obviously sets  are grand ornate and wonderfully  larger-than-life which is fitting for a  story that was first staged play plus  you can't deny that Bob Hoskins Maggie  Smith and Dustin Hoffman are absolutely  perfect in their parts and the score by  John Williams is pure magic alien 3 when  first-time director David Fincher was  hired for alien 3 the movie was already  behind schedule  there wasn't a finished script and the  studio had lost several million dollars  on the project were still venture was  severely hampered by micromanaging  producers Fox and when the film clocked  in around three hours long the studio  forced him to cut about 30 minutes of  material as a result of that bad  experience ventured disavowed the 19  to film but he wasn't the only one who  hated alien 3 fans and James Cameron are  still furious about the deaths of Hicks  and Newt and most critics panned the  film with many saying it was style over  substance it's true that alien 3 puts a  lot of emphasis on atmosphere but at the  same time that's what makes it one of  the most fascinating movies of Fincher's  storied career set in a dreary wasteland  that screams Mad Max this third  installment is a dark grungy and  beautifully barren cinematic nightmare  Sigourney Weaver is at the top of her  game here playing a more cynical Ripley  who bravely comes to terms with her  inevitable fate true the xenomorph  effects look a little janky and the  forest edits take away from the overall  story but despite the drawbacks alien 3  is a visually brilliant world of  criminal monks and fiery hell escapes a  film that could have been the perfect  ending for a landmark series national  treasure 2004's national treasure was  described by critics as rancid cinematic  cheese with almost nothing recommended  but that's more than a little bit harsh  the film follows Nicolas Cage as  Benjamin Franklin gates a historian who  learns that there's a treasure map  hidden on the back of the Declaration of  Independence  unfortunately fellow treasure hunter Ian  Howe played by Sean Bean decides to  steal the Declaration forcing Ben and  his sidekick Riley to break into the  National Archives and get their hands on  the document first with the Declaration  in hand Ben sets out across the stomping  grounds of America's founding fathers  solving riddles and doing his best to  avoid a barrage of bullets he it's easy  to argue that cage was miscast as the  swashbuckling lead of a popcorn  adventure flick and somebody definitely  should have realized there were 56  signers of the Declaration of  Independence not 55 but national  treasure really shines what our heroes  are puzzling over codes and trying to  piece together clues  there's invisible ink hidden ciphers and  glasses invented by Benjamin Franklin  not to mention conspiracies involving  the Masons and the Knights Templar plus  Sean Bean Diane Kruger and Harvey Keitel  are all fun to watch as they help and  hinder cage in his quest to find the  world's biggest pile of gold  Robocop remaking a classic is always in  that pill battle especially when that  classic is a beloved sci-fi masterpiece  like Robocop Jose Padilla is 21st  century remake doesn't stand a chance  against Paul Verhoeven's 1987 original  but the 2014 version is definitely a  film with its own style and its own  ideas Joel Kinnaman stars as Alex Murphy  a Detroit cop who's been mortally  wounded by a car bomb with his corpse  mangled beyond recognition  he's the perfect candidate for an  experimental program that turns him into  a cybernetic super cop naturally there  is more going on with the Robocop  program than meets the eye  and soon enough Murphy sets out to bring  down the big bads dead or alive  admittedly Kinnaman often has even less  emotion than Peter Weller and the pg-13  rating means that the originals  over-the-top violence has been toned  down considerably in the name of making  a buck still the 2014 film is  surprisingly thoughtful focusing on the  widespread use and danger of drone  warfare and the increasing  militarization of urban police forces  the satirical news sequences from the  original have been swapped out with Fox  News style segments featuring an angry  samuel l.jackson and there's an  interesting update to Alex Murphy's  character arc in the 1987 film Murphy  starts off as a cold blooded cyborg who  slowly regains his humanity but padilla  puts a nice twist on the story with an  emotional Murphy becoming more and more  machine as the movie goes on losing his  ability to connect with others the film  also grapples with heady concepts like  free will and while it lags in a few  places  it features an amazing in the dark  shootout and a truly horrific sequence  where Alex Murphy sees what's left of  his body after a near-fatal explosion  it's never going to replace the original  but  I'd buy that for a dollar  the last Boy Scout Tony Scott's the last  Boy Scout had an infamously troubled  production thanks in part to the  enormous number of rewrites that  screenwriter Shane Black was forced to  make as a result were left with a movie  that's messy disjointed and nowhere near  as tight as blacks other work like  Lethal Weapon or the nice guys that said  while it might be a little jumbled the  last Boy Scout is a fascinating slice of  action cinema a movie full of insane  little moments involving car bombs  helicopter blades and foul mouthed cat  puppets it starts off with one of the  most compelling openings you'll ever see  in an action movie with a drug-addled  football player pulling a pistol in the  middle of a game from there we're  introduced to our heroes cynical private  investigator Joe Hallenbeck played by  Bruce Willis and has been quarterback  Jimmy Dix  played by Damon Wayans investigate the  brutal murder of Dix's girlfriend a  dancer played by Halle Berry as the duo  dig deeper into the case they uncover a  plot involving gambling in the NFL but  what's really important here is that  Shane blacks dialogue is still as clever  as ever and the dynamic between Willis  and Wayans is crackling with frenemy  energy  leather pants  yeah it has more than a bit of a problem  when it comes to its female characters  but on the positive side Taylor Negron  shows up as one of the very best Shane  black villains and were treated to a  scene in which Bruce Willis murders a  dude with the palm of his hand  anaconda with a 38 percent approval  rating on Rotten Tomatoes 1997 s  anaconda is generally considered pretty  awful critic Mick LaSalle slammed the  film by saying anaconda is about a snake  that eats everybody that about says it  all timeout London's review added that  the snake doesn't just eat people it  screams Steve Newton of the Georgia  Strait also piled on writing just to  show early on how much danger these  folks are in we get to see the film's  titular star squeeze a black panther so  tight that one of its eyeballs pop out  we're not really seeing why this is a  problem that's exactly the kind of stuff  that makes anaconda so amazing if you go  into the movie expecting something  classy you might walk away disappointed  but if you're expecting the best b-movie  ever then you left the time of your life  it's the kind of movie where a guy  falling from a waterfall gets snatched  out of the air by a snake hanging from a  tree branch and where you can watch a  guy gets swallowed alive from inside the  Anaconda itself in addition to the  killer creatures there's Jon Voight as  Paul Cerrone a mad man who chews up all  the scenery in sight every one of his  lines isn't over-the-top Jim and he  strangles somebody with his own legs  before dousing Jennifer Lopez with a  bucket of monkey blood what's not the  love Snow White and the Huntsman 2012's  Snow White and the Huntsman has been  accused of being putrid and seemingly  endless strange and solace and  underwhelming with a woeful script but  it's worth watching for the visuals  alone directed by Rupert Sanders this  gritty fantasy has images on par with  something you might see at a movie  directed by Guillermo del Toro there's  the battle where Knights fight an army  of soldiers made of glass or the scene  when Queen Ravenna bathes herself in a  milky brew right after munching out of  bird's hearts a White Stag bursts into a  thousand butterflies  and after turning into a flock of Ravens  ravenna crawls from a pit of black ooze  surrounded by the fluttering of dying  birds with sequences like these Snow  White in the Huntsman is equal parts  dreamland and nightmare fuel and it  doesn't hurt that Charlize Theron throws  herself into playing the world's most  evil witch cranking up the volume and  turning on the terror every time she  walks on screen while throne is  wonderfully nuts his Queen Ravenna  Kristen Stewart the most underrated  actress in Hollywood anchors the film  with a much more realistic and  empathetic performances Snow White even  Chris Hemsworth steps up his acting game  and while it would have been preferable  if they used actual little people the  dwarfs are played by some of the very  best British character actors like Bob  Hoskins Nick Frost and Ian McShane and  they lighten things up every time they  appear sure it doesn't compare to the  Walt Disney classic but then it's not  really trying to this is a snow-white  movie that's more about the dark side of  fairy tales the blood the mud the horror  and the Wonder Clash of the Titans with  reviews like New York magazines which  said it isn't a train wreck a train  wreck would be memorable  2010's Clash of the Titans wrapped up a  miserable 28 percent rating on Rotten  Tomatoes and went down as a mistake of  mythological proportions even the star  Sam Worthington said the movie let down  some people but honestly if the critics  didn't like it that might have been  because they lost their sense of fun  Clash of the Titans is a good old  fashioned b-movie a throwback to a time  when Hollywood made adventure flicks  that could charm you with crazy  creatures over-the-top set pieces and a  whole lot of imagination the story  itself is pretty simple  mankind is getting sick of the gods  bossing them around so they decide it's  time to rebel naturally the gods don't  approve  so they threatened to unleash the  monstrous Kraken the human King doesn't  sacrifice his daughter  desperate the King turns to a demigod  named Perseus asking him to find a way  to kill the Beast  along the way Perseus encounters a  murderous Medusa some oversized  scorpions and a trio of witches ease ifs  between towers on a Pegasus teams up  with a group of desert dwelling Djinn  and is forced to deal with all-powerful  gods like Liam Neeson's Zeus and Hades  played by ray Fiennes who turned the  Shakespeare up to 11 mix that in with a  bunch of monsters and magic and you can  see how Clash of the Titans is exactly  the kind of movie that might inspire the  next charge Lucas to make the next Star  Wars thanks for watching click the  looper icon to subscribe to our YouTube  channel plus check out all this cool  stuff we know you love to  [Music]

